      "costUsd": 0.0120555,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here, with the integral from minus two to one, of x squared. The primitive is x cubed over three, so we need one third, minus negative two cubed over three. It's like reading a thermometer on a frosty morning. The change is the later reading, minus the earlier one, and the earlier one can be below zero. Before we work it out, look at the graph. X squared is never negative, so every strip sits above the axis. Should the answer be positive, or negative? Here's a slip that happens all the time. Negative two cubed is minus eight, and someone writes one third minus eight thirds, which is minus seven thirds. A negative answer, from a region that's all above the axis. Something has gone wrong. So how do we evaluate definite integrals without losing signs?"
    },
