      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 169,
      "outputTokens": 1177,
      "costUsd": 0.0106775,
      "pronunciationText": "What if the limits come the other way round, from one down to minus two? The rule still says F of the top, minus F of the bottom. Now the top is minus two, so it's minus eight thirds, minus one third, which is minus three. Swapping the ends swaps the order of the subtraction, so the answer changes sign. On the thermometer, that's the evening, cooling from one degree back to minus two, a change of minus three. That's how a backwards integral is defined. The integral from b to ay is minus the integral from a to b. Picture the strips running from right to left. Every strip's width is negative, so every strip flips its sign. And if both ends are the same, there are no strips at all, so the integral is zero."
