      "inputTokens": 193,
      "outputTokens": 1473,
      "costUsd": 0.013353499999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Signs get trickier when the primitive has a minus sign of its own. Find the integral from zero to pi, of sine x. A primitive is minus cos x. At pi, that's minus cos pi, which is minus, minus one, so it's one. At zero, it's minus cos zero, which is minus one. So the answer is one, minus, bracket, minus one, which is two. Sine x is above the axis from zero to pi, so a positive answer is what we expect. One minus one, giving zero, is the slip the brackets prevent. The same care works with an inside. The integral from zero to pi over four, of sine two x, has primitive minus a half cos two x. At the top that's zero, and at the bottom it's minus a half. So the answer is zero, minus, bracket, minus a half, which is a half."
    },
